# Pre-launch status: no production deployment, no live users

**This platform has no production deployment.** There are no live customers,
no production data, and no existing users depending on current behavior.
Treat this as ground truth whenever assessing risk for a proposed change.

## What this means for risk assessment

- **"Breaking existing customers" is not a real risk yet.** Don't inflate the
  risk of a spec-conformance fix, RLS policy change, or default-behavior
  correction by imagining live orgs/users who depend on the current (possibly
  wrong) behavior — there are none.
- **Prefer clean fixes over migration scaffolding.** Feature flags, per-org
  opt-in toggles, dual code paths, deprecation windows, and backward-compat
  shims exist to protect *live* users through a transition. Absent live
  users, default to the simplest correct implementation (one behavior, one
  code path) unless the toggle is itself a deliberate, requested product
  feature (e.g. plan-tier differentiation) rather than a safety net.
- **Resolve spec-vs-code conflicts by picking the correct answer, not by
  preserving both.** When a spec (or two specs) disagree with the
  implementation or each other, prefer amending the spec/implementation to
  match the intended behavior over adding a toggle purely to avoid
  "regressing" the disputed behavior.
- **This does not relax test rigor.** Every acceptance criterion still needs
  real, executed test coverage (see
  [.cursor/rules/test-execution-integrity.mdc](.cursor/rules/test-execution-integrity.mdc)) — skipping
  verification because "no one uses this yet" is never acceptable. Only
  *migration-safety* scaffolding (flags, dual paths, deprecation periods) is
  what this rule says to skip — not verification, and not security/RLS
  correctness.
- **Re-check this rule if the project reaches production.** Once a real
  deployment with live users exists, this doctrine no longer applies and
  standard backward-compatibility discipline (see
  [.cursor/rules/linear-automation.mdc](.cursor/rules/linear-automation.mdc) for the PR/merge lifecycle)
  resumes. Update or remove this rule at that point.
